{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>Do not defile yourselves in any of these things.</b> The implication is that they should not reach the final extreme of evil such that the land becomes defiled — [and this would only happen] if they defile themselves in <em>all</em> of these; and afterward it says, ״וְלֹא תַעֲשׂוּ מִכֹּל הַתּוֹעֵבוֹת״ — the implication being that even with <em>some</em> of them the land becomes defiled. And it appears proper to explain that for this reason two types of punishment were mentioned: namely, the vomiting of the land, and the cutting off of the souls that commit [these acts]. And first he warned: do not defile yourselves in all of these, to do all of them — for in all of these the nations defiled themselves and reached the final extreme such that the land becomes defiled and its iniquity is visited upon it and it becomes like a sick person who vomits, and it vomits out its inhabitants because its measure has been filled, and the way of a full measure is to spill over and vomit, for it can hold no more. And therefore they were warned that the same should not happen to you as well, for then <em>andarlemusya</em> comes and kills good and evil alike, for thus the silent ones and those abandoned [to death] went forth into exile along with Jeremiah and his company. And afterward he warns them: ״וּשְׁמַרְתֶּם אַתֶּם״ and so forth, ״וְלֹא תַעֲשׂוּ מִכֹּל הַתּוֹעֵבוֹת״ — even some of them you shall not do; for all the abominations the men of the land committed and they were subject to the punishment of the land's vomiting, but you — if you do some of them, the land will not vomit you out; you will not be included in the punishment of vomiting, but in any case there is another punishment, namely ״כִּי כָּל אֲשֶׁר יַעֲשֶׂה מִכֹּל הַתּוֹעֵבוֹת״ — even some of them; even though strict justice does not warrant destroying good and evil together, in any case ״וְנִכְרְתוּ הַנְּפָשׁוֹת הָעוֹשׂוֹת״ — at the very least [those souls are cut off]. Therefore ״וּשְׁמַרְתֶּם אֶת מִשְׁמַרְתִּי לְבִלְתִּי עֲשׂוֹת מֵחֻקּוֹת הַתּוֹעֵבוֹת״ — even some of them. And an interpretation close to this I saw in a new book."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "The term אַנְדַּרְלָמוּסְיָא (andarlemusya) transliterated literally; it appears to be a loanword, possibly from a European vernacular, connoting general destruction or upheaval. The phrase הֶחָרָשׁ וְהַמַּסְגֵּר refers to the language of Jeremiah 25:24 (הֶחָרָשׁ וְהַמַּסְגֵּר), understood in rabbinic tradition as those who were left behind, silent and abandoned, in the land at the time of the Babylonian exile."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:37:22.404101227Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}